Bert Bos was born in The Hague, Netherlands, in 1963. He studied mathematics at the University of Groningen and earned a PhD there on rapid user interface development.

## Argo and style sheets

In the early 1990s, Bos built Argo, one of the early web browsers. He used it to test his own proposal for style sheets, a way to separate how a page looks from what it says. When Håkon Wium Lie proposed Cascading Style Sheets in 1994, Bos joined him, and the two combined their ideas. He is considered the co-creator of CSS.

## A life in the CSS Working Group

- **1996:** Joined the World Wide Web Consortium (W3C) to work on CSS.
- **1996–1998:** Wrote the CSS1 and CSS2 specifications with Lie.
- **Later:** Chaired the W3C CSS Working Group and then became its W3C staff contact, coordinating the group's work.

He also co-wrote *Cascading Style Sheets: Designing for the Web* with Lie. The book's third edition was made with HTML and CSS and turned into a printed book by the Prince formatter. It showed that the language they designed for screens could also lay out a book.

Bos works from the W3C office in Sophia Antipolis, France.

